   Synthetic data with 10 curated fraud scenarios and 5 curated false positives is a *designed* demo. Real claims data is messier, noisier, and full of edge cases the demo doesn't cover. The gap between "demo works" and "production works" is where most enterprise AI projects die.

2. **Vision model reliability on medical documents.**
   Claude Haiku 4.5 detecting font inconsistencies and erasure marks is plausible but not proven at scale. What's the false positive rate on legitimately low-quality scans from rural clinics? If it flags 30% of real documents, examiners will turn it off.

3. **The autonomous investigation loop is the most impressive feature and the most fragile.**
   "It knows when it doesn't have enough evidence and loops back" — this is great when it works. When it fails, it either loops forever (cost problem) or prematurely declares sufficiency (quality problem). The prototype showing this working "twice" doesn't tell you anything about the distribution of behaviors in production.

